<p>Running a short-term rental in Houston is legal, but since January 1, 2026, every property inside city limits that is rented for less than 30 consecutive days must be registered with the City of Houston, carry a current certificate and pay hotel occupancy taxes. On top of the city rules, your deed restrictions, insurance policy and the numbers themselves decide whether a short-term rental beats a regular lease.</p>
<p>This guide covers the Houston STR ordinance, the 17% hotel tax, HOA and deed restriction issues, insurance and how to compare STR and long-term returns honestly.</p>

<h2>Houston's short-term rental ordinance at a glance</h2>
<p>Houston City Council adopted its first short-term rental ordinance (Ordinance No. 2025-322, Chapter 28, Article XXIII of the City Code) on April 16, 2025. Registration opened in fall 2025, compliance became mandatory on January 1, 2026, and the city began active enforcement in spring 2026. The <a href="https://www.houstontx.gov/ara/str.html">City of Houston short-term rental page</a> run by the Administration and Regulatory Affairs Department has the portal, rules and checklist.</p>
<p>The ordinance defines a short-term rental as a dwelling unit, or any part of one, rented or offered for rent for less than 30 consecutive days. Hotels, bed and breakfasts, boarding homes and seller leasebacks after a home sale are excluded. Each unit needs its own certificate, including individual condos and apartments in the same building.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>What the city asks for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Certificate of registration</td><td>One per unit, valid for one year, renewed annually; not transferable to a buyer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fee</td><td>$275 per unit per year, non-refundable, plus a city administrative fee that adjusts annually ($33.10 in 2025)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hotel tax</td><td>Proof of HOT registration, or proof the platform remits HOT on your behalf</td></tr>
<tr><td>Emergency contact</td><td>Someone reachable by phone 24/7 who can respond within one hour of being notified</td></tr>
<tr><td>Training</td><td>A pre-approved human trafficking awareness course</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ownership and restrictions</td><td>Proof of ownership or owner authorization, plus a declaration that the rental does not violate HOA rules, deed restrictions or lease terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>On-site postings</td><td>The certificate and the 24-hour contact names and numbers, posted inside the front entrance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Listings</td><td>The registration number must appear on platform listings</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Operating rules and penalties</h3>
<p>Registered hosts must follow the city's noise, trash, building and fire codes, and cannot advertise a rental as an event space. The city's published answers to frequently asked questions say there is no maximum occupancy limit, no cap on the number of rentals one owner can hold and no insurance mandate.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fines:</strong> $100 to $500 per violation, and each day a violation continues counts separately.</li>
<li><strong>Revocation:</strong> for certain criminal convictions, repeated noise or nuisance violations, building code violations, false information or unpaid hotel tax. A revoked owner cannot reapply for a year, and if three or more of one owner's certificates are revoked within 24 months, the city can move against the rest.</li>
<li><strong>Delisting:</strong> platforms must remove a listing within 10 business days after the city notifies them it lacks a valid registration.</li>
<li><strong>Appeals:</strong> you can request a hearing within 20 days of a denial or revocation notice.</li>
</ul>
<p>These rules apply only inside Houston city limits. Suburbs such as Pasadena, Sugar Land or League City, and unincorporated Harris County, set their own rules, so check with the city or county where the property sits.</p>

<h2>Hotel occupancy tax on Houston short-term rentals</h2>
<p>Texas treats a short-term rental like a hotel room for tax purposes. The <a href="https://comptroller.texas.gov/taxes/hotel/">Texas Comptroller</a> applies the 6% state hotel occupancy tax to stays of fewer than 30 consecutive days, and local taxes stack on top. Inside Houston city limits the combined rate is 17%:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Tax</th><th>Rate</th><th>Collected by</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>State of Texas</td><td>6%</td><td>Texas Comptroller</td></tr>
<tr><td>City of Houston</td><td>7%</td><td>Houston First Corporation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harris County</td><td>2%</td><td>Harris County Tax Office</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harris County-Houston Sports Authority</td><td>2%</td><td>Harris County Tax Office</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>What the platforms collect, and what you still owe</h3>
<p>According to Houston First, Airbnb has collected and remitted the City of Houston tax for its hosts since July 1, 2019, and Vrbo (HomeAway) since January 1, 2020, with no opt-out. The Comptroller says a platform that agrees to collect state tax handles it; otherwise the owner must collect and remit. Each platform publishes which Texas taxes it collects, so check the tax page for every channel you use.</p>
<p>Any booking that a platform does not cover is your responsibility, including direct bookings through your own website and bookings on platforms that do not remit. That means registering with each agency, charging guests the tax, and filing returns. Houston First and the Harris County Tax Office both use quarterly reports, due April 30, July 31, October 31 and January 31, and Houston First expects a report every quarter even when no tax is due. Guests who stay 30 or more consecutive days without a break in payment are generally exempt as permanent residents.</p>

<h2>HOA rules and deed restrictions</h2>
<p>Houston has no zoning, but deed restrictions and HOA covenants do much of the same work, and many subdivisions and condominiums restrict rentals. Read the restrictions before you buy, not after.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>"Residential use only" may not be enough to ban STRs.</strong> In <em>Tarr v. Timberwood Park Owners Association</em> (2018), the Texas Supreme Court held that a covenant limiting a lot to residential purposes did not, on its own, prohibit short-term vacation rentals.</li>
<li><strong>Express rental restrictions are a different story.</strong> Covenants that set minimum lease terms or expressly prohibit short-term leasing can be enforceable, and associations sometimes amend restrictions to add them, following the amendment process their documents require.</li>
<li><strong>The city application makes you declare compliance.</strong> If you register a property whose restrictions prohibit STRs, you risk both an HOA enforcement action and a false-information problem with the city.</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/blog/texas-hoa-laws/">Texas HOA laws</a> covers how associations can enforce restrictions and what notice they must give.</p>

<h2>Insurance for a Houston short-term rental</h2>
<p>The city does not require proof of insurance, but your lender and your own balance sheet will. A standard homeowners policy is written for owner occupancy and may limit or exclude coverage when a home is rented to paying guests. Ask your agent, in writing, whether your policy covers short-term rental use, guest injuries and guest damage, and consider a dedicated short-term rental or commercial landlord policy.</p>
<p>Platform host protection programs are not a substitute for a policy you control. In the Houston area, also price windstorm and flood coverage separately, because hurricanes and street flooding are part of the risk profile, and flood is excluded from standard policies. Our <a href="/blog/how-to-analyze-a-rental-property/">rental analysis guide</a> walks through pricing those costs.</p>
<p>Basic safety gear matters more with a rotating cast of guests who do not know the house. Working smoke and carbon monoxide alarms on every level are the minimum.</p>
